  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": null,
      "what": "The authors evaluated three land use and land cover (LULC) classification workflows for Jagatsinghpur district and its surrounding area in Odisha, India, and used the top-performing workflow to project LULC changes through 2050. Using a hybrid CA-ANN-Markov model, the study shows that built-up areas will expand 2.2-fold from approximately 298 km² in 2017 to 655 km² by 2050, primarily consuming cropland, which drops from 2128 km² to 1677 km². Among the tested methods, the pre-classified ESRI Global LULC product achieved the highest agreement at 87.44% (κ: 0.79), outperforming Random Forest on Sentinel-2 (78.08%, κ: 0.65) and Maximum Likelihood Classification on Landsat (45.06%, κ: 0.31).",
      "why": "The study quantifies the substantial gap in classification accuracy between off-the-shelf global LULC products and standard remote sensing workflows for local projection modeling in coastal India. It also provides specific spatial projections of cropland loss and urban expansion to support future coastal flood risk and hydrological assessments."
    }
  },
